  <info>
    <title>The &systemd; journal</title>
    <!-- can be changed via merge in the assembly -->
    <!--add author's email address-->
    <meta name="maintainer" content="souvik.sarkar@suse.com" its:translate="no"/>
    <abstract>
      <!-- can be changed via merge in the assembly -->
      <para>
        &systemd; features its own logging system called
        <emphasis>journal</emphasis>. The journal itself is a system service
        managed by &systemd;&mdash;<literal>systemd-journald.service</literal>.
      </para>
    </abstract>
  </info>
  <para>
    The <literal>systemd-journald</literal> service is enabled and started by
    default. On &productnameshort;, it stores log data persistently in
    <filename>/var/log/journal/</filename>, so entries survive a reboot, and
    forwards a copy of every message to
    <systemitem class="daemon">rsyslog</systemitem> (see
    <xref linkend="logging-logs-location"/>). To query the journal, use the
    <command>journalctl</command> command (see
    <xref linkend="logging-journalctl-usage"/>). For a deeper look at the
    journal and everything <command>journalctl</command> can do, see the
    dedicated article <citetitle>Mastering System Logs: From
    systemd-journald to journalctl</citetitle>.
  </para>
</topic>
